1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,490
Your heart beats about a hundred
thousand times a day.

2
00:00:03,930 --> 00:00:04,790
Over a lifetime,

3
00:00:05,430 --> 00:00:08,640
that's around three billion
beats, without a single day

4
00:00:08,650 --> 00:00:09,450
off.

5
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:13,070
It pumps around seven thousand
litres of blood every day,

6
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:16,790
enough to fill a small swimming
pool every few months.

7
00:00:17,667 --> 00:00:19,747
But when it fails, there is no
backup.

8
00:00:20,487 --> 00:00:22,587
Heart disease is the world's
biggest killer.

9
00:00:23,333 --> 00:00:26,683
So for seventy years, scientists
and surgeons have

10
00:00:26,693 --> 00:00:30,123
tried to do something
incredible: build a machine that
can

11
00:00:30,133 --> 00:00:31,393
replace the human heart.

12
00:00:33,067 --> 00:00:36,367
Your heart is really two pumps,
side by side.

13
00:00:37,083 --> 00:00:40,373
The right side sends tired,
oxygen-poor blood

14
00:00:40,383 --> 00:00:41,183
to the lungs.

15
00:00:41,723 --> 00:00:44,893
The left side, the stronger one,
pushes fresh,

16
00:00:44,903 --> 00:00:47,623
oxygen-rich blood out to the
whole body.

17
00:00:48,333 --> 00:00:50,453
Four valves act like one-way
doors,

18
00:00:51,253 --> 00:00:53,063
so blood only flows forwards.

19
00:00:53,073 --> 00:00:56,463
And a natural pacemaker sends an
electrical spark

20
00:00:56,473 --> 00:00:57,833
to time every beat.

21
00:00:58,583 --> 00:01:02,033
Copying that with metal and
plastic, inside a living

22
00:01:02,043 --> 00:01:02,613
body,

23
00:01:02,623 --> 00:01:05,793
for years, turned out to be one
of the hardest problems

24
00:01:05,803 --> 00:01:06,603
in medicine.

25
00:01:07,417 --> 00:01:10,737
And unlike skin or bone, the
heart barely repairs itself.

26
00:01:11,297 --> 00:01:14,407
In an adult, only about one
percent of heart muscle

27
00:01:14,417 --> 00:01:17,747
cells are replaced each year, so
damage from a

28
00:01:17,757 --> 00:01:19,717
heart attack is largely
permanent.

29
00:01:21,567 --> 00:01:24,597
The first step was a machine
that could take over from the
heart

30
00:01:24,607 --> 00:01:27,847
for a few hours, so surgeons
could operate on it.

31
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:31,890
An American surgeon, John
Gibbon, spent nearly twenty

32
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:35,070
years building one, partly
funded by IBM

33
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:35,880
engineers.

34
00:01:36,917 --> 00:01:40,067
His obsession began in the early
nineteen thirties,

35
00:01:40,077 --> 00:01:43,337
when he sat all night beside a
woman dying from a blood

36
00:01:43,557 --> 00:01:46,987
clot in her lungs, and imagined
a machine that could take over

37
00:01:46,997 --> 00:01:48,337
while surgeons removed it.

38
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,290
His machine spread the blood in
thin films over metal screens

39
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:55,790
inside a chamber of oxygen,
doing the work of

40
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:58,440
the lungs, while a pump did the
work of the heart.

41
00:01:59,083 --> 00:02:01,973
On the sixth of May, nineteen
fifty three,

42
00:02:01,983 --> 00:02:05,333
he used it during an operation
to repair a hole in the heart of

43
00:02:05,343 --> 00:02:08,443
an eighteen year old student,
Cecelia Bavolek.

44
00:02:08,823 --> 00:02:11,463
The machine kept her alive for
twenty six minutes.

45
00:02:12,263 --> 00:02:13,443
She recovered completely.

46
00:02:14,333 --> 00:02:16,073
Open-heart surgery was born.

47
00:02:16,553 --> 00:02:19,523
But surgeons still dreamed of
something that could replace a

48
00:02:19,533 --> 00:02:20,333
heart for good.

49
00:02:20,833 --> 00:02:24,063
In nineteen fifty eight, Swedish
doctors implanted

50
00:02:24,073 --> 00:02:27,283
the first pacemaker, a small
device that

51
00:02:27,293 --> 00:02:30,743
sends electrical pulses to keep
a slow heart beating

52
00:02:30,753 --> 00:02:31,553
steadily.

53
00:02:31,893 --> 00:02:33,713
Its first patient, Arne Larsson,

54
00:02:34,353 --> 00:02:37,623
went on to receive twenty six of
them, and outlived both

55
00:02:37,633 --> 00:02:38,533
its inventors.

56
00:02:40,150 --> 00:02:43,190
In December nineteen sixty
seven, in Cape Town,

57
00:02:43,610 --> 00:02:46,940
South Africa, surgeon Christiaan
Barnard performed

58
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:48,910
the first human heart
transplant.

59
00:02:49,917 --> 00:02:53,267
His patient, Louis Washkansky,
lived for

60
00:02:53,277 --> 00:02:54,157
eighteen days.

61
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:56,760
The new heart itself worked
well.

62
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:00,450
He died of pneumonia, because
the drugs that stopped his

63
00:03:00,460 --> 00:03:03,790
body rejecting it had also
weakened his defences against

64
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:04,600
infection.

65
00:03:05,250 --> 00:03:08,420
It took until the nineteen
eighties, and a new drug

66
00:03:08,430 --> 00:03:11,640
called cyclosporine, for
transplants to become

67
00:03:11,650 --> 00:03:12,890
truly reliable.

68
00:03:13,590 --> 00:03:16,920
Today, more than four thousand
heart transplants happen

69
00:03:16,930 --> 00:03:19,010
every year in the United States
alone.

70
00:03:19,833 --> 00:03:22,253
But there's a problem that has
never gone away:

71
00:03:22,873 --> 00:03:24,233
there are never enough donor
hearts.

72
00:03:24,913 --> 00:03:26,713
Many patients die while waiting.

73
00:03:27,417 --> 00:03:30,627
That's why engineers kept
chasing the dream of a
mechanical

74
00:03:30,637 --> 00:03:31,437
heart.

75
00:03:32,733 --> 00:03:35,453
In April nineteen sixty nine, in
Houston,

76
00:03:35,813 --> 00:03:39,083
surgeon Denton Cooley implanted
the first total artificial

77
00:03:39,093 --> 00:03:42,416
heart in a human, designed by
Domingo Liotta.

78
00:03:42,917 --> 00:03:46,237
It kept the patient, Haskell
Karp, alive for sixty

79
00:03:46,247 --> 00:03:49,137
four hours, until a donor heart
was found.

80
00:03:49,617 --> 00:03:52,537
It was meant as a bridge, not a
permanent replacement.

81
00:03:53,583 --> 00:03:56,873
In Salt Lake City, a team at the
University of

82
00:03:56,883 --> 00:03:57,673
Utah,

83
00:03:57,683 --> 00:04:01,013
including the engineer Robert
Jarvik, built a

84
00:04:01,023 --> 00:04:02,023
stronger design:

85
00:04:02,663 --> 00:04:03,883
the Jarvik seven.

86
00:04:04,417 --> 00:04:07,787
Two plastic pumping chambers
replaced the heart's two lower

87
00:04:07,797 --> 00:04:08,597
chambers.

88
00:04:08,877 --> 00:04:12,287
Inside each, a flexible
diaphragm was pushed by

89
00:04:12,297 --> 00:04:15,037
puffs of compressed air,
squeezing blood out.

90
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:19,060
On the second of December,
nineteen eighty two,

91
00:04:19,070 --> 00:04:22,340
a sixty one year old dentist
named Barney Clark became

92
00:04:22,350 --> 00:04:25,420
the first person to receive one
as a permanent

93
00:04:25,430 --> 00:04:26,230
replacement.

94
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,140
He lived for a hundred and
twelve days.

95
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:32,930
But he was tied by tubes to an
air compressor the size

96
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:36,100
of a washing machine, and
suffered strokes and infections.

97
00:04:37,167 --> 00:04:40,187
The world watched with a mix of
wonder and unease.

98
00:04:40,527 --> 00:04:43,367
Was this extending life, or just
extending suffering?

99
00:04:44,333 --> 00:04:47,598
Another patient, William
Schroeder, lived for six hundred
and

100
00:04:47,608 --> 00:04:50,983
twenty days with a Jarvik seven,
and even

101
00:04:50,993 --> 00:04:52,533
went on a fishing trip.

102
00:04:53,250 --> 00:04:55,730
Both implants were led by the
same surgeon,

103
00:04:56,050 --> 00:04:57,320
William DeVries.

104
00:04:57,330 --> 00:05:00,250
But the strokes kept coming, and
in nineteen

105
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:00,920
ninety,

106
00:05:00,930 --> 00:05:04,370
US regulators withdrew approval
for the Jarvik heart.

107
00:05:05,983 --> 00:05:09,153
Doctors realised that most
failing hearts don't need to be

108
00:05:09,163 --> 00:05:10,163
replaced completely.

109
00:05:10,923 --> 00:05:13,773
Often, just the left side, the
main pump,

110
00:05:13,783 --> 00:05:15,023
is too weak.

111
00:05:15,750 --> 00:05:17,990
So they built smaller devices to
help it.

112
00:05:18,370 --> 00:05:21,690
A left ventricular assist
device, or LVAD,

113
00:05:21,850 --> 00:05:25,280
is a pump about the size of a
fist, attached to the

114
00:05:25,290 --> 00:05:26,090
heart.

115
00:05:26,250 --> 00:05:29,560
The first ones copied the heart,
with pumping sacs that

116
00:05:29,570 --> 00:05:33,030
squeezed and relaxed, and wore
out within a few years.

117
00:05:33,730 --> 00:05:36,110
Switching to spinning rotors
made them smaller,

118
00:05:36,690 --> 00:05:38,430
quieter and far longer lasting.

119
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:41,413
Modern LVADs don't beat at all.

120
00:05:41,933 --> 00:05:45,063
A tiny spinning rotor, often
levitated by

121
00:05:45,073 --> 00:05:48,103
magnets so it never wears out,
pushes

122
00:05:48,113 --> 00:05:49,393
blood in a smooth,

123
00:05:50,013 --> 00:05:51,413
continuous stream.

124
00:05:52,250 --> 00:05:53,910
That leads to something
astonishing:

125
00:05:54,850 --> 00:05:57,010
many people with these pumps
have no pulse.

126
00:05:57,670 --> 00:06:00,090
A doctor feeling their wrist
would find nothing,

127
00:06:00,450 --> 00:06:01,950
even though they're perfectly
alive.

128
00:06:02,917 --> 00:06:06,267
A cable runs through the skin to
a controller and batteries

129
00:06:06,277 --> 00:06:08,477
worn on a belt or in a bag.

130
00:06:08,937 --> 00:06:12,307
Patients can go home, walk, and
even return to

131
00:06:12,317 --> 00:06:13,117
work.

132
00:06:13,250 --> 00:06:16,490
Tens of thousands of people have
now lived with an LVAD,

133
00:06:16,710 --> 00:06:18,310
some for more than ten years.

134
00:06:18,890 --> 00:06:22,160
The former US vice president
Dick Cheney lived with

135
00:06:22,170 --> 00:06:25,510
one for about twenty months,
before a heart transplant.

136
00:06:27,317 --> 00:06:30,097
Total artificial hearts are
still used too,

137
00:06:30,617 --> 00:06:33,457
as a bridge for people whose
whole heart has failed.

138
00:06:33,997 --> 00:06:37,467
The best known today, SynCardia,
is a descendant

139
00:06:37,477 --> 00:06:38,937
of the Jarvik seven.

140
00:06:39,667 --> 00:06:41,927
More than two thousand have been
implanted.

141
00:06:42,347 --> 00:06:44,987
And instead of a
washing-machine-sized
compressor,

142
00:06:45,447 --> 00:06:48,717
patients can now carry a
portable driver in a

143
00:06:48,727 --> 00:06:49,527
backpack.

144
00:06:50,083 --> 00:06:51,563
The newest designs go further.

145
00:06:52,123 --> 00:06:55,083
The BiVACOR heart, from an
Australian inventor,

146
00:06:55,703 --> 00:06:59,013
Daniel Timms, replaces both
pumps with a single

147
00:06:59,023 --> 00:07:02,303
spinning disc, floating in a
magnetic field.

148
00:07:03,083 --> 00:07:04,043
It has no valves,

149
00:07:04,683 --> 00:07:07,323
nothing to rub, and nothing to
wear out.

150
00:07:07,843 --> 00:07:11,333
In twenty twenty four, the first
patients received it in the

151
00:07:11,343 --> 00:07:12,143
United States.

152
00:07:12,917 --> 00:07:16,207
Fittingly, the first implant
took place at the Texas

153
00:07:16,217 --> 00:07:19,107
Heart Institute in Houston,
where Denton

154
00:07:19,117 --> 00:07:22,467
Cooley had implanted the first
artificial heart fifty five

155
00:07:22,477 --> 00:07:23,677
years earlier.

156
00:07:24,397 --> 00:07:27,487
Timms had started the project as
a PhD student

157
00:07:27,497 --> 00:07:30,217
in Brisbane, more than twenty
years before.

158
00:07:31,083 --> 00:07:34,293
In twenty twenty five, an
Australian man in his

159
00:07:34,303 --> 00:07:37,103
forties became the first to
leave hospital with one,

160
00:07:37,463 --> 00:07:40,473
living for more than a hundred
days on a titanium heart,

161
00:07:40,483 --> 00:07:42,603
before receiving a donor heart.

162
00:07:44,233 --> 00:07:46,133
Other researchers are trying
different routes:

163
00:07:46,713 --> 00:07:49,743
hearts from genetically modified
pigs, and heart

164
00:07:49,753 --> 00:07:52,613
tissue grown from a patient's
own stem cells.

165
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:56,670
In twenty twenty two, a man in
Maryland lived

166
00:07:56,680 --> 00:07:59,280
for two months with a
gene-edited pig heart,

167
00:07:59,780 --> 00:08:02,340
the first such transplant in a
living person.

168
00:08:03,250 --> 00:08:06,270
His name was David Bennett, and
he was fifty

169
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:06,900
seven.

170
00:08:06,910 --> 00:08:09,820
A second patient received a pig
heart the following year,

171
00:08:09,830 --> 00:08:11,930
and lived for about six weeks.

172
00:08:12,833 --> 00:08:15,943
And researchers have printed
small, beating patches

173
00:08:15,953 --> 00:08:19,363
of heart tissue using 3D
printers loaded with

174
00:08:19,373 --> 00:08:20,173
living cells.

175
00:08:21,083 --> 00:08:22,723
None of these is perfect yet.

176
00:08:23,223 --> 00:08:25,913
But each year, people who would
once have died are living

177
00:08:25,923 --> 00:08:26,333
longer,

178
00:08:26,343 --> 00:08:28,003
at home, with their families.

179
00:08:29,733 --> 00:08:32,583
The human heart beats three
billion times without

180
00:08:32,593 --> 00:08:33,393
stopping.

181
00:08:33,833 --> 00:08:36,793
Engineers have spent seventy
years trying to match it.

182
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:38,940
They haven't beaten nature yet.

183
00:08:39,280 --> 00:08:41,860
But they've learned how to keep
a human being alive,

184
00:08:42,420 --> 00:08:44,160
with a heart made by human
hands.
